   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to exempting hot tubs or spas\nwith safety covers from certain provisions of law",
    "basePrintNo": "S7858",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7858-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §378, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7858\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to exempting hot tubs or\nspas with safety covers from certain provisions of law\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo clarify building safety codes relating to hot tub or spa safety\ncovers.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. The Executive law is amended by amending section 378 of the\nExecutive Law.\n\nSection 2. This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nFor years, lockable hot tub safety covers have demonstrated the highest\nlevel of protection by isolating the hot tub at the source, not just the\narea surrounding the hot tub like a fence. With a lockable cover\nsecured, access by a small child is not possible.  Additionally, the\nvast energy savings provided by these covers also creates a strong\nincentive on behalf of the user to quickly secure the cover whenever the\nhot tub is not in use, whereas fence gates are much less secure.\n\nA recent change in the state's building code could be interpreted to\ntreat hot tubs and spas as swimming pools with respect to safety barri-\ners, allowing for fencing as the primary safety barrier for hot tubs and\nspas. This legislation would make a technical change to the hot tub and\nspa building code statute to clarify lockable hot tub and spa safety\ncovers are the primary means of a safety barrier.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe bill shall take effect immediately.\n",
